INSERT INTO public.qb_questions (subject_id, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, points, tags) VALUES
('11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A yarn count conversion table shows that Denier and Tex are both DIRECT systems, related by Denier = Tex × 9. If a technologist mistakenly believes this constant is ''Denier = Tex × 9'' should instead be applied as ''Tex = Denier × 9'' (inverting the relationship), and calculates a yarn''s Tex from a known 150 Denier value, what WRONG Tex value would this error produce, versus the CORRECT value?', '["Wrong (inverted) calculation: 150×9=1,350 Tex. Correct calculation: 150/9≈16.67 Tex — the inverted formula overstates Tex by roughly 81-fold, an enormous, easily-detectable error if the technologist sanity-checks against typical yarn Tex ranges","Both the correct and incorrect calculation methods produce identical results, making this described error scenario technically impossible to actually occur","Wrong (inverted) calculation: 150/9≈16.67 Tex. Correct calculation: 150×9=1,350 Tex — this option reverses which formula is actually correct, describing the true correct relationship as the error and vice versa","The Denier-to-Tex conversion constant is not 9 at all, making both described calculation methods in this question fundamentally based on an incorrect premise"]'::jsonb, 'Wrong (inverted) calculation: 150×9=1,350 Tex. Correct calculation: 150/9≈16.67 Tex — the inverted formula overstates Tex by roughly 81-fold, an enormous, easily-detectable error if the technologist sanity-checks against typical yarn Tex ranges', 'Since Denier = Tex × 9, correctly solving for Tex requires DIVIDING by 9, not multiplying: Tex = Denier/9 = 150/9 ≈ 16.67 Tex. The inverted (wrong) approach of multiplying instead (150×9=1,350 Tex) produces a drastically, obviously wrong result — real yarn Tex values for typical apparel yarns are generally well under 100 Tex, so a result of 1,350 Tex should immediately trigger a sanity-check red flag, illustrating the practical value of having a rough intuitive sense of typical value RANGES for common textile units, not just memorizing formulas mechanically.', 3, '{"denier-tex-inversion-error"}'::text[]),
('11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A yarn producer''s technical report distinguishes ''nominal count tolerance'' (e.g., ±3% for a specific yarn grade) from ''count CV%'' (evenness variation ALONG the yarn''s length). If a yarn''s AVERAGE count tests exactly on-target (0% deviation from nominal) but its count CV% is unusually high (poor evenness), does this yarn satisfy a buyer''s ''count tolerance ±3%'' specification?', '["Yes, unconditionally — since the AVERAGE count is exactly on nominal target, the yarn automatically satisfies any count tolerance specification regardless of its internal evenness/CV% variation","Not necessarily — a buyer''s count tolerance spec, if interpreted strictly, may require that count measured at ANY given point/sample along the yarn stays within the tolerance band, not just that the AVERAGE across a large sample happens to land on target — a yarn with high CV% could have PERFECT average count while individual segments swing well outside the ±3% band, meaning average-count compliance alone doesn''t guarantee true point-by-point tolerance compliance, a genuinely important distinction depending on how the buyer''s specification is precisely defined and tested","Count CV% and count tolerance measure mathematically identical properties, making this described scenario (good average, poor CV%) technically impossible to actually occur in any real yarn","A yarn''s count tolerance specification is always satisfied automatically regardless of CV%, since CV% is calculated as a completely separate, unrelated statistical metric with zero bearing on tolerance compliance interpretation"]'::jsonb, 'Not necessarily — a buyer''s count tolerance spec, if interpreted strictly, may require that count measured at ANY given point/sample along the yarn stays within the tolerance band, not just that the AVERAGE across a large sample happens to land on target — a yarn with high CV% could have PERFECT average count while individual segments swing well outside the ±3% band, meaning average-count compliance alone doesn''t guarantee true point-by-point tolerance compliance, a genuinely important distinction depending on how the buyer''s specification is precisely defined and tested', 'This is a genuinely subtle specification-interpretation insight connecting the earlier count-tolerance and CV%/evenness discussions — a PERFECT average count (0% deviation) is mathematically compatible with WIDE variation at individual points along the yarn (some segments well above nominal, others well below, averaging out to exactly on-target) if CV% is poor — depending on whether a buyer''s ''count tolerance'' specification is meant to apply to the AVERAGE measurement or to EVERY individual test point, a yarn with excellent average but poor CV% could either technically PASS (if only average is checked) or genuinely FAIL (if point-by-point consistency is the real intent) the same nominal specification — highlighting why precise specification language (and clarity on what exactly is being tested/verified) matters, not just the headline tolerance percentage number.', 3, '{"count-tolerance-vs-CV-interpretation"}'::text[]),
('11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A spinning mill blends two cotton varieties by WEIGHT: 70% Variety X (fiber length 28mm) and 30% Variety Y (fiber length 32mm). Assuming a simple weighted average approximation for the blend''s average fiber length, what is the predicted blend average length?', '["30mm (simple unweighted average of 28 and 32), incorrectly ignoring the unequal 70/30 weight split","29.2mm, correctly weighted: (28×0.70)+(32×0.30) = 19.6+9.6 = 29.2mm","28mm, incorrectly using only the majority variety''s length and ignoring Variety Y''s contribution entirely","32mm, incorrectly using only the longer variety''s length regardless of its smaller 30% weight share"]'::jsonb, '29.2mm, correctly weighted: (28×0.70)+(32×0.30) = 19.6+9.6 = 29.2mm', 'Weighted average = (28×0.70)+(32×0.30) = 19.6+9.6 = 29.2mm — this directly parallels the earlier weighted-average fiber-strength mixing calculation, requiring the SAME correct method (weighting each component by its actual proportion of the total mixing, not treating both varieties as equal 50/50 contributors) — using a simple unweighted average (30mm) is a common, systematically incorrect shortcut whenever a blend''s components aren''t in exactly equal proportions.', 3, '{"blend-weighted-average-length"}'::text[]),
('11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A yarn manufacturer''s cost sheet shows raw fiber cost is 62% of total yarn production cost, with the remaining 38% split across processing (28%) and overhead/profit (10%). If raw cotton price INCREASES by 15% (with all other costs unchanged), what is the resulting PERCENTAGE increase in the yarn''s TOTAL production cost?', '["15% increase in total cost, incorrectly applying the raw material''s own percentage increase directly to the total cost figure","9.3% increase in total cost, correctly calculated as 0.62 (fiber''s share of total cost) × 0.15 (fiber''s own price increase) = 0.093, i.e., 9.3 percentage points added to total cost","62% increase in total cost, incorrectly using the fiber''s COST SHARE percentage as if it were the total cost INCREASE percentage","38% increase in total cost, incorrectly using the NON-fiber cost share (100%-62%) as the answer instead of correctly calculating the fiber-share-weighted price increase"]'::jsonb, '9.3% increase in total cost, correctly calculated as 0.62 (fiber''s share of total cost) × 0.15 (fiber''s own price increase) = 0.093, i.e., 9.3 percentage points added to total cost', 'Since raw fiber is only 62% of TOTAL cost (not 100%), a 15% increase in fiber price only increases TOTAL cost by 0.62×0.15=0.093, i.e., 9.3% — NOT the full 15%, since the other 38% of cost (processing, overhead/profit) is unaffected by the fiber price change — this is a common, important costing calculation: a raw material price change must be weighted by that material''s SHARE of total cost to correctly calculate its impact on the FINAL total cost, not applied directly as if the raw material were 100% of the cost structure.', 3, '{"cost-share-weighted-price-impact"}'::text[]);
